A||||5||FIEROCIOUS||Fuel gage question||||1||
Z||000000||FIEROCIOUS||07-26-2000||03:57 AM||||My fuel gage has been broke since I bought the car it reads 1/4 tank when it is absolutely empty and 1/4 past full when full.<P>Whats up?<P>What do I need to fix the gage or the tank sender? <P>(God, don't say the tank sender)||||reg||1
Z||000001||Standard||07-26-2000||05:28 AM||||I would say.. fill the tank to full, pull the needle off the gauge, and put it back on so it's pointed at the full mark.  No need to get all high-tech about it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mike -- Golden Valley, MN<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 147,000 miles and counting!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||07-26-2000||10:01 AM||||It's the gage not the sender. Just pull the needle off when tank is full and replace it pointing to full. Use a thick glue like 5-minute epoxy. If you use a thin glue like superglue you risk it running down the shaft and gluing the shaft to the gage faceplate.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 07-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||b lo 0||07-26-2000||10:30 AM||||A good friend of mine had the same problem with his '93 Gran Prix (yes the one I raced).  I reccomend the same thing Standard and Oreif do.  Did the same thing to my '77 C10 a few years back.  I still have this problem on my '90 Wrangler-just too busy on the Fiero to fix it.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT (in progress)  <BR>titled ICE-plates B LO 0 <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery/ice.gif">   <BR>Go to <A HREF="http://www.audioimagery.i-p.com" TARGET=_blank>www.audioimagery.i-p.com</A>  !!!<p>[This message has been edited by b lo 0 (edited 07-26-2000).]||||reg||7
Z||000004||Master_Sushi||07-26-2000||11:29 AM||||Wow. I was just about to post a thread about the same problum  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. Mine is also way off like FIEROCIOUS's.||||reg||1
